course: books
lesson: 69
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XI, part 5: «Like Summer Tempests Came His Tears» (Mole the washerwoman and the badgers that never were)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Mole comes tumbling in, very pleased with himself. He found Toad's old washerwoman dress drying by the kitchen fire, put it on with the bonnet and shawl, and walked boldly up to the sentries at Toad Hall: «Good morning, gentlemen! Want any washing done to-day?» «We don't do any washing on duty,» they said. «Or any other time?» said Mole — and some of the stoats turned quite pink.
explain: When the Sergeant told the «good woman» to run away, Mole said it wouldn't be her running away soon. Then, as a washerwoman who washed for Mr. Badger, he «warned» them: tonight a hundred badgers with rifles would attack from the paddock, six boatloads of Rats would land in the garden, and the fierce Death-or-Glory Toads would storm the orchard!
explain: Mole ran off, crept back along a ditch and peeped through the hedge. The stoats were in a flap, running all ways, falling over each other, the Sergeant sending parties off to far corners and then fetching them back — and grumbling that the weasels would be feasting in the warm while they were cut to pieces in the cold.
explain: «You silly ass, Mole! You've been and spoilt everything!» cries Toad. But Badger says Mole has «more sense in your little finger than some other animals have in the whole of their fat bodies. Good Mole! Clever Mole!» Toad is wild with jealousy and can't see what was clever. After lunch Badger takes forty winks, and Mole kindly lets Toad tell all his adventures — with plenty of things that might have happened, if only he'd thought of them in time.
fact: Mole got the idea in the kitchen, where he found «that old washerwoman-dress that he came home in yesterday, hanging on a towel-horse before the fire» (a towel-horse = a wooden frame for drying cloths).
fact: «Good morning, gentlemen!» says I, very respectful. «Want any washing done to-day?» The sentries: «Go away, washerwoman! We don't do any washing on duty.» Mole: «Or any other time?» — a cheeky hint that they are dirty.
fact: «Some of the stoats turned quite pink» — they were embarrassed.
fact: The Sergeant: «Now run away, my good woman, run away! Don't keep my men idling and talking on their posts.» Mole: «it won't be me that'll be running away, in a very short time from now!»
fact: Mole's made-up story: «My daughter, she washes for Mr. Badger»; «A hundred bloodthirsty badgers, armed with rifles, are going to attack Toad Hall this very night, by way of the paddock.»
fact: Also: «Six boatloads of Rats, with pistols and cutlasses, will come up the river» and land in the garden (a cutlass = a short curved sword).
fact: And «a picked body of Toads, known as the Die-hards, or the Death-or-Glory Toads, will storm the orchard» (picked = specially chosen; die-hards = fighters who never give up).
fact: Peeping through the hedge, Mole saw them «running all ways at once, and falling over each other, and every one giving orders to everybody else and not listening».
fact: The Sergeant kept «sending off parties of stoats to distant parts of the grounds, and then sending other fellows to fetch 'em back again».
fact: The stoats grumbled that the weasels would «stop comfortably in the banqueting-hall, and have feasting and toasts and songs», while they guarded «in the cold and the dark».
fact: Badger: «Mole, I perceive you have more sense in your little finger than some other animals have in the whole of their fat bodies. You have managed excellently... Good Mole! Clever Mole!» (I perceive = I see)
fact: Toad «felt exceedingly jealous of Mole», because it was exactly what he would have liked to do himself, «if only he had thought of it first, and hadn't gone and overslept himself».
fact: Lunch was «bacon and broad beans, and a macaroni pudding»; then Badger took «forty winks» (a short nap) with a handkerchief over his face.
fact: The Rat went on: «Here's-a-belt-for-the-Rat, here's-a-belt-for-the-Mole...» with «every fresh accoutrement» (piece of equipment).
fact: Toad told Mole his adventures and «rather let himself go»: much of it was «what-might-have-happened-had-I-only-thought-of-it-in-time-instead-of-ten-minutes-afterwards».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «ate» = did brilliantly; «salty» = jealous, annoyed; «canny» (North East England) = clever; «lekker» (South Africa) = great; «gurt» (Bristol) = very big.
exercise: Where did Mole find the washerwoman's dress? | Drying on a towel-horse by the kitchen fire | In Toad's room | In the river | At the gaoler's daughter's house
exercise: What did Mole put on with the dress? | The bonnet and the shawl | Goggles | Rat's suit | A policeman's helmet
exercise: What did Mole first say to the sentries? | «Good morning, gentlemen! Want any washing done to-day?» | «Who comes there?» | «Hands up!» | «Let me in at once, I'm Mr. Toad, and this is my house!»
exercise: «We don't do any washing on duty.» «Or any other time?» What is Mole hinting? | That they never wash at all — they are dirty | That they are very clean | That he will come back tomorrow | That he knows where the soap is
exercise: How did some of the stoats react? | They turned quite pink | They laughed | They fired their guns | They took Mole prisoner
exercise: Who told Mole, «Now run away, my good woman»? | The Sergeant in charge | The Chief Weasel | A ferret | A young stoat
exercise: For whom did Mole say his «daughter» washed? | Mr. Badger | Mr. Toad of Toad Hall | The Chief Weasel | The gaoler at the castle
exercise: How many badgers did Mole say would attack? | A hundred | Six or seven | Only four of them | A thousand, with drums
exercise: From which side would the badgers come, said Mole? | The paddock | The river bank | The old orchard | The kitchen chimney
exercise: How would the Rats come? | Six boatloads up the river, landing in the garden | Through the passage | By train | On horseback through the front gate
exercise: «Pistols and cutlasses.» What is a cutlass? | A short curved sword | A gun | A small boat | A bread-knife
exercise: What were the fierce Toads called? | The Death-or-Glory Toads | The Poop-poops | The Washerwomen | The Ingenious Toads of Toad Hall
exercise: What did Mole do after giving his warning? | Ran off, then crept back to peep | Went straight home to the Rat's house by the river | Joined the stoats | Knocked at the front door
exercise: What did Mole see the stoats doing? | Running all ways at once | Sleeping soundly at their posts | Playing cards | Eating cake
exercise: What was the Sergeant doing? | Sending parties off and then fetching them back | Writing a letter | Running away himself | Shouting at the Chief Weasel
exercise: What were the stoats grumbling about? | The weasels feasting while they guarded | Their pay from the Chief Weasel | Their guns | The noise of the river
exercise: What did Toad say to Mole? | «You've been and spoilt everything!» | «Clever Mole!» | «Thank you!» | «How funny you look!»
exercise: What did Badger say Mole had more of in his little finger? | Sense | Strength | Curly hair | Lots of money
exercise: How did Toad feel when Badger praised Mole? | Wild with jealousy | Proud of his friend | Sleepy | Glad
exercise: Why was Toad jealous? | He wished he'd thought of it first | Mole had taken his dress without asking | Mole was taller | Badger gave Mole a gift
exercise: Which of these was for lunch? | Bacon, broad beans, macaroni pudding | Cold pie and pickles | Fried ham and eggs with toast | Stew
exercise: «I'm just going to take forty winks.» What does that mean? | Have a short nap | Wink forty times | Count to forty | Look at forty things
exercise: What did the Rat start handing out after lunch? | Belts | Swords | Pistols | Sandwiches
exercise: What kind of adventures did Toad tell Mole in the garden? | Partly made up | Only true ones | Only sad ones | None at all
test: Freya: «Mole just went for a jolly, nothing to do with the plan, proper waste of time.» Put it right. | He scared and scattered the sentries | Right, it was only a game | He went shopping in the village for the banquet | He went to free the weasels
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «The hundred badgers were real.» | False: Mole made them up | True: Badger sent for them | True: they came by the paddock | False: there were a thousand
test: Why did the stoats believe the «washerwoman»? | She said she washed for Mr. Badger | She had a gun | She showed them a letter from the Chief Weasel | She cried
test: Lexi, salty (jealous) like Toad: «Badger's got favourites now, Mole ate (did brilliantly) for nothing.» What does Badger value in Mole? | Good sense | His washing | His strength | His singing
test: Why does Toad think Mole «spoilt everything»? | He thinks it warned the stoats | He wanted the dress back for himself to wear | He hates stoats | He wanted lunch
test: What would happen to the sentries if they believed the attack was coming from three sides? | They'd be spread out and jumpy | They'd go home | They'd be braver and fight harder than ever | They'd lock the passage
test: Callum, canny (clever) bairn: «why are the stoats moaning about the weasels?» Best answer? | The weasels get the party, the stoats the cold | They hate washing and the weasels make them do it | The weasels owe them money | They're hungry for broad beans
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, Toad escaped prison dressed as a washerwoman. How is Mole's use of the dress different? | Toad used it to escape; Mole to trick the enemy | Mole used it to escape from the stoats too | Mole wore it to wash clothes | Mole wore it to a party
test: Looking back: in chapter III, Mole went into the Wild Wood alone and was terrified. How has he changed? | He now tricks armed sentries boldly | He is still terrified of them and hides in the ditch | He never leaves home | He asks Rat to come
test: Looking back: in chapter I, Mole grabbed the sculls and tipped the boat over. How is today's Mole different? | Then silly pride; now a clever idea for friends | He still tips boats over whenever he rows | He is the same | He can't row
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Badger said that when Toad is quiet and submissive, «then he's at his artfullest». What does Badger now praise in Mole? | Real sense, used for his friends | Talking a lot and telling stories | Swagger | Sleepiness
test: Looking back: who said in chapter IX «I've an idea that you'll feel a lot better when you've got something jotted down» to cheer up a friend? | Mole, to the Rat | Badger, to Toad | Toad, to Rat | Otter, to Portly
test: Leo: «Toad's stories to Mole were all true facts, he's a legit (honest) bloke now.» Put it right. | Many were «what-might-have-happened» | Yes, every one of them was perfectly true | He told none | He told only Mole's stories
test: Why did Mole let Toad tell his adventures in the garden? | Kindness: Mole was a good listener | Mole wanted to learn tricks | Badger ordered it | Rat was too busy with belts to listen
test: ★★★ «Or any other time?» Why did the stoats turn pink and not laugh? | They understood the joke was about them | They were cold | They were angry with the Sergeant for shouting | They were sunburnt
test: ★★★ Mole's imaginary army came from three places. Which place did he NOT name? | The chimney | The paddock | The river and garden | The orchard
test: ★★★ «More sense in your little finger than some other animals have in the whole of their fat bodies.» Whom is Badger quietly teasing? | Toad | The Rat | The Sergeant | Otter
test: ★★★ If each of the «six boatloads» held 8 Rats, how many imaginary Rats were coming? | 48 | 14 | 68 | 86
exam: Identify the article of attire assumed by the Mole in his expedition to Toad Hall. | The washerwoman's dress, bonnet and shawl | Toad's motoring habiliments, comprising goggles and gaiters | The Rat's suit | A sweep's attire with brushes
exam: Ascertain the nature of the intelligence imparted by the Mole to the sentries. | A fictitious account of an imminent attack | A true account of the secret passage beneath the pantry | An offer of laundry services only | A message from the Chief Weasel
exam: It is given that the Badger commended the Mole. Select the response which most accurately characterises Toad's disposition upon this occasion. | He was wild with jealousy | He was gratified on his friend's behalf | He was indifferent | He was asleep
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the Mole's observation of the sentries from behind the hedge? | They were in great confusion | They stood motionless at their posts | They had deserted entirely | They were feasting in the banqueting-hall
